    <string name="advice_title">Tips &amp; alerts</string>
    <string name="advice_pressure_falling_fast">Pressure is falling fast (−%1$s hPa/h) — weather may turn bad quickly, thunderstorm possible. Consider shelter or descent.</string>
    <string name="advice_pressure_falling">Pressure is falling (−%1$s hPa/h) — weather may deteriorate.</string>
    <string name="advice_pressure_rising">Pressure is rising — weather is likely improving.</string>
    <string name="advice_alt_acclimatize">Above 2500 m altitude sickness is possible — ascend gradually and watch how you feel.</string>
    <string name="advice_alt_high">Above 3000 m: limit daily sleeping-altitude gain to 300–500 m, drink more water.</string>
    <string name="advice_alt_very_high">Above 4000 m: high risk of altitude sickness. Headache, nausea, dizziness — descend immediately.</string>
    <string name="advice_fast_ascent">Fast ascent — a slower pace helps acclimatization.</string>
    <string name="advice_hydration">At altitude the body loses water faster — drink regularly.</string>
    <string name="advice_spo2_low">SpO₂ %1$s%% is lowered — take a break and rest. If you feel unwell, descend.</string>
    <string name="advice_spo2_very_low">SpO₂ %1$s%% is low — stop, rest, warm up. If it doesn\'t rise or you feel bad — descend and seek help.</string>
    <string name="advice_hr_high">Pulse %1$s bpm at altitude — take a pause to recover.</string>
    <string name="advice_gps_weak">Weak GPS signal — altitude accuracy is reduced. Move to open sky.</string>
    <string name="advice_disclaimer">Tips are informational only, not medical advice.</string>

